WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:03.531
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! I'm

2
00:00:03.532 --> 00:00:12.604
Faye Moss. Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History , the talk show featuring interviews with famous people from

3
00:00:12.605 --> 00:00:24.505
the past. Today's guest was a lawyer and a political fighter who used nonviolence to help India, then a British colony, become an independent

4
00:00:24.506 --> 00:00:49.215
country. Known as the Mahatma—or Great Soul—please welcome Mohandas Gandhi!

5
00:00:49.216 --> 00:00:53.219
MOHANDAS GANDHI: Hello, Faye. Please call

6
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.798
me Gandhi. The Mahatma title always made

7
00:00:57.799 --> 00:01:05.109
me uncomfortable. FAYE MOSS: You are so modest! And I know you were also very shy, especially

8
00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.240
as a child. What else can you tell us about your

9
00:01:08.241 --> 00:01:16.711
early life? MOHANDAS GANDHI: I was born in Porbandar, India, in 1869, when India was part

10
00:01:16.712 --> 00:01:23.392
of the British Empire. My family was Hindu, and my mother was very devout

11
00:01:23.393 --> 00:01:29.026
and fasted regularly. My father was a local government leader, and we

12
00:01:29.027 --> 00:01:35.665
lived comfortably. India, however, was a poor country divided by caste

13
00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:43.264
and religion. There were many poor people and untouchables—outcasts who performed the dirtiest

14
00:01:43.265 --> 00:01:48.871
jobs. India had a large Hindu population, but there were also many

15
00:01:48.872 --> 00:01:54.109
Muslims. Though the two groups didn't always get along, I had Muslim friends.

16
00:01:54.110 --> 00:02:06.008
In fact, even though I was a vegetarian for religious reasons, I tried eating meat with a brave and strong Muslim friend, hoping to be more like him! Alas,

17
00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:09.325
it didn't work. FAYE MOSS:

18
00:02:09.326 --> 00:02:13.739
At age 19 you went to London to study law.

19
00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.988
By then you were married and had a son.

20
00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:23.388
MOHANDAS GANDHI: Yes, our parents had arranged the marriage, which was customary

21
00:02:23.389 --> 00:02:28.221
then. My wife and son stayed in India while I was away.

22
00:02:28.222 --> 00:02:38.873
Three years later I returned to India to practice law, but because of my shy personality, I had little success. In

23
00:02:38.874 --> 00:02:46.924
1893 I was offered a position in South Africa, which was also part of the British Empire. I

24
00:02:46.925 --> 00:02:56.459
moved there with my family for a yearlong job, but God led me to stay there for 21 years. FAYE MOSS:

25
00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.346
What was the job? MOHANDAS

26
00:02:58.347 --> 00:03:03.141
GANDHI: I performed legal services for Indian

27
00:03:03.142 --> 00:03:13.106
immigrants. Many Indians had immigrated there for work, and we encountered unfair treatment from the white settlers who ruled South Africa.

28
00:03:13.107 --> 00:03:18.020
We had to pay an annual tax, which many couldn't afford. We

29
00:03:18.021 --> 00:03:23.627
also had to be fingerprinted and carry registration cards. FAYE

30
00:03:23.628 --> 00:03:29.823
MOSS: One of your firsthand experiences with discrimination was a turning point in your life.

31
00:03:29.824 --> 00:03:38.938
MOHANDAS GANDHI: Yes. I was on a train in a first-class compartment when a white man did not want to sit near

32
00:03:38.939 --> 00:03:43.166
me. The railway officials told me to move to another

33
00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:49.709
compartment. When I refused, they forced me off the train.

34
00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:57.883
I spent the night in a cold train station, thinking about the deep disease of prejudice.

35
00:03:57.884 --> 00:04:04.422
I decided then to fight it, even if I had to suffer hardship in the process. I

36
00:04:04.423 --> 00:04:14.322
began organizing workers' strikes and protests, and encouraged civil disobedience to protest unfair laws. FAYE MOSS:

37
00:04:14.323 --> 00:04:18.875
Why was nonviolent protest so important to you?

38
00:04:18.876 --> 00:04:25.706
MOHANDAS GANDHI: Hindus believe in nonviolence, and I had read religious texts and writings from great

39
00:04:25.707 --> 00:04:31.902
thinkers that confirmed to me that nonviolence was the best way to fight oppression.

40
00:04:31.903 --> 00:04:37.905
I called this satyagraha , which means "force of the

41
00:04:37.906 --> 00:04:43.277
soul." We didn't hate our oppressors; we wanted to win them over.

42
00:04:43.278 --> 00:04:53.131
FAYE MOSS: Your campaign worked! Those unjust laws against Indians in South Africa were changed. What did you do

43
00:04:53.132 --> 00:04:58.358
next? MOHANDAS GANDHI: My family and I returned to

44
00:04:58.359 --> 00:05:08.547
India in 1915. But my experiences with discrimination in South Africa led me to question more than ever the role of the British in

45
00:05:08.548 --> 00:05:15.942
my homeland. How could 100,000 British rule 315 million

46
00:05:15.943 --> 00:05:21.164
Indians? FAYE MOSS: You had some very specific goals to prepare for

47
00:05:21.165 --> 00:05:30.325
independence, didn't you? MOHANDAS GANDHI: I wanted Indians to become more self-reliant, growing our own food and

48
00:05:30.326 --> 00:05:37.863
spinning our own clothes. I also wanted to improve the lives of the poor and end

49
00:05:37.864 --> 00:05:45.902
discrimination against untouchables. After all, how could we expect the English to stop oppressing us when we Indians

50
00:05:45.903 --> 00:05:55.387
oppressed untouchables? And I preached unity between Hindus and Muslims. We had to be united if we were to rule

51
00:05:55.388 --> 00:06:00.139
ourselves! FAYE MOSS: Peaceful protests also played a big part

52
00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:08.458
in your strategy. MOHANDAS GANDHI: Yes, but sadly, horrible violence broke out

53
00:06:08.459 --> 00:06:14.356
in Amritsar in 1919. A British general ordered soldiers to fire

54
00:06:14.357 --> 00:06:19.155
on a peaceful protest. Hundreds were killed, and a

55
00:06:19.156 --> 00:06:26.301
thousand were wounded. Then, to my horror, Indians became violent, killing

56
00:06:26.302 --> 00:06:34.903
some police officers. To protest this violence, I fasted. Fasting wasn't a tactic I could use against

57
00:06:34.904 --> 00:06:43.800
my enemies. It only worked on my supporters, who worried so much when I fasted that they changed their behavior for the

58
00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:49.378
good. They feared I would die—and sometimes I almost

59
00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:54.784
did. I fasted often—my longest fast was three weeks.

60
00:06:54.785 --> 00:07:02.242
FAYE MOSS: Your most famous protest was the Salt March of 1930. Can you tell us more about

61
00:07:02.243 --> 00:07:11.183
that? MOHANDAS GANDHI: The British taxed salt, so I organized a 24-day, 240-mile march

62
00:07:11.184 --> 00:07:18.687
to the sea. Once there, we gathered our own salt to avoid the tax, an act that broke

63
00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:23.010
the law. Thousands of us, including me,

64
00:07:23.011 --> 00:07:31.551
were arrested. But importantly, the British realized that they couldn't rule India without the cooperation of the Indian

65
00:07:31.552 --> 00:07:38.803
people. The British held a conference on the situation in India, and I traveled to London to participate.

66
00:07:38.804 --> 00:07:40.681
FAYE MOSS: How did

67
00:07:40.682 --> 00:07:46.995
that go? MOHANDAS GANDHI: I'm afraid I returned to India without an agreement on

68
00:07:46.996 --> 00:07:53.813
my country's independence. When World War II started, Britain drew India into the fighting without

69
00:07:53.814 --> 00:08:02.401
our consent. I started the "Quit India" campaign, encouraging Indians to act as if we were no longer under British

70
00:08:02.402 --> 00:08:07.935
rule. I was arrested again, along with many other Indian

71
00:08:07.936 --> 00:08:16.361
leaders. Two years later I was released. Nearly six years of my life were spent in Indian jails.

72
00:08:16.362 --> 00:08:22.783
FAYE MOSS: When the war ended, the British were finally ready to give India its

73
00:08:22.784 --> 00:08:26.107
independence! MOHANDAS GANDHI: It wasn't

74
00:08:26.108 --> 00:08:35.068
so simple though. The Muslim minority didn't trust the Hindu majority to rule them fairly, so they wanted their own state

75
00:08:35.069 --> 00:08:45.268
of Pakistan. When India gained independence on August 15, 1947, the country was partitioned into India and

76
00:08:45.269 --> 00:08:55.705
Pakistan. Independence was bittersweet. I had failed to keep India united! And the ensuing crisis was

77
00:08:55.706 --> 00:08:58.404
dreadful! FAYE MOSS: I can tell the rest of

78
00:08:58.405 --> 00:09:04.733
that story. Thank you so much for joining us! We'll let you get back to history

79
00:09:04.734 --> 00:09:10.277
now. MOHANDAS GANDHI:

80
00:09:10.278 --> 00:09:17.922
Thank you, Faye. FAYE MOSS: After the partition, Muslims in India moved to Pakistan, and Hindus

81
00:09:17.923 --> 00:09:26.946
in Pakistan moved to India. When horrible violence broke out, Gandhi held prayer meetings with Hindus and Muslims, vowing

82
00:09:26.947 --> 00:09:35.414
to fast until death. Luckily the violence stopped. But on January 30, 1948, another act

83
00:09:35.415 --> 00:09:43.013
of violence occurred. Gandhi was assassinated by a man upset over Gandhi's support for peaceful

84
00:09:43.014 --> 00:09:46.254
Hindu-Muslim relations. The Mahatma

85
00:09:46.255 --> 00:09:54.725
was 78. Gandhi's nonviolent protests inspired other civil rights leaders such as Dr. Martin Luther King and Nelson

86
00:09:54.726 --> 00:10:02.078
Mandela. The International Day of Nonviolence is celebrated each year on his birthday, October

87
00:10:02.079 --> 00:10:07.907
2. That's all for today's show. I'll see you again next time!

